  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "NPR reports that while Flock ALPR cameras have drawn public backlash, privacy experts argue that AI-powered real-time crime center software like Axon's Fusus poses broader surveillance concerns by aggregating ALPRs, drones, body cams, 911, gunshot detection, and private camera feeds into a single interface. Use of Fusus has more than doubled since Axon's 2024 acquisition, with police touting efficiency gains while experts call for legislative guardrails.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "real-time crime centers have not just ALPR cameras and sensors, but video analytics and drones and essentially a way to create a visual single-pane-of-glass vision of what's going on in a city, with time machine capabilities where you can go back and investigate crime.",
      "paragraph_idx": 12
    },
    {
      "quote": "The number of law enforcement agencies using the tool has more than doubled since then, according to Axon.",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
    },
    {
      "quote": "What happens if there is a government that wants to silence a particular group or even a particular person? And you just built the architecture of surveillance that makes that super-easy",
      "paragraph_idx": 24
    }
  ],
